WEBVTT

00:00:04.480 --> 00:00:09.600
Hello. My name is Philippe, and I'm excited to present to you this paper called LMs get lost in

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:10.960
multiturn conversation.

00:00:11.120 --> 00:00:15.345
This is in collaboration with my dear friends and colleague, Hiroaki

00:00:15.345 --> 00:00:18.225
Hayashi at Salesforce and Jennifer Neville at MSR.

00:00:19.505 --> 00:00:25.105
So let me walk you through a little bit of what the motivation of this work is, which is a conversation

00:00:25.105 --> 00:00:28.110
Hiro and I had in September 2024,

00:00:28.350 --> 00:00:30.750
we were talking about programming language benchmarks

00:00:30.910 --> 00:00:35.790
and how they were sort of saturated. Like, if you looked at benchmarks like human eval,

00:00:37.230 --> 00:00:40.270
you know, a lot of models were getting 90 plus accuracy on them,

00:00:41.155 --> 00:00:46.355
and we felt like, okay, the models at the time were not not very good yet at programming

00:00:46.355 --> 00:00:47.475
language tasks.

00:00:48.035 --> 00:00:48.675
And

00:00:49.075 --> 00:00:54.275
I wasn't sure why I'm not a programming language person, and Hero is an expert on this topic, so we we

00:00:54.275 --> 00:00:55.955
kind of, like, looked at some examples together.

00:00:57.460 --> 00:01:01.700
And if you're not familiar with human eval, it's an OpenAI dataset,

00:01:02.020 --> 00:01:07.140
and it has samples like here on the left where, basically, you're given a docstring of

00:01:07.140 --> 00:01:12.265
a function. Here, the example is a function called below zero where you'll be given a list of

00:01:12.265 --> 00:01:13.785
deposits with withdrawals

00:01:14.345 --> 00:01:19.385
and the bank account that starts with zero, and then your task is to detect if at any point the balance goes below zero

00:01:19.385 --> 00:01:24.505
and return true. Otherwise, it should return false. Right? And when you look at this problem, you

00:01:24.505 --> 00:01:25.465
might have two intuitions.

00:01:25.910 --> 00:01:31.350
The AI intuition is that, you know, okay. Like, the reason why this benchmark is easy is because the task is too simple,

00:01:31.350 --> 00:01:35.910
and that real coding tasks are actually, you know, harder than this in terms of complexity.

00:01:36.550 --> 00:01:40.230
And then if you're more of an HCI minded person, you might think, oh,

00:01:41.205 --> 00:01:46.245
but is this actually how people would write a prompt? Perhaps, like, there's just a misalignment between how this

00:01:46.245 --> 00:01:48.805
benchmark is set up and how actual people would use it.

00:01:49.845 --> 00:01:55.045
And we started playing around with, you know, okay. How would people actually, you know, wanna implement this function approach

00:01:55.045 --> 00:01:59.050
this, you know, if they went to, you know, their favorite chat conversational interface?

00:01:59.610 --> 00:02:02.250
And we came up with this idea of a sharded instruction.

00:02:02.570 --> 00:02:07.610
The sharded instruction here on the right is an equivalent instruction that has the exact same information as

00:02:07.610 --> 00:02:09.370
the instruction here on the left that you can see,

00:02:10.125 --> 00:02:12.605
except that it's been split into six shards here.

00:02:12.845 --> 00:02:15.245
And each shard essentially is a piece of information

00:02:15.485 --> 00:02:16.205
that,

00:02:16.365 --> 00:02:20.365
you know, you might reveal in a single turn such that when you simulate a conversation,

00:02:20.445 --> 00:02:25.520
you're not gonna reveal all the information needed to do the task at the start, but you might, you know, start by

00:02:25.520 --> 00:02:30.560
saying, hey. I need to write a function called below zero that finds if accounts are ever negative. And

00:02:30.560 --> 00:02:35.600
then, you know, the model might answer, and then you might say, oh, by the way, the input is a list of ints that represent transaction

00:02:35.600 --> 00:02:35.920
amounts.

00:02:36.435 --> 00:02:41.155
And then in the third turn, you might say, oh, by the way, we let's assume that the initial balance is zero,

00:02:41.395 --> 00:02:45.395
etcetera. And you can kind of see that, like, if you do this, you kind of underspecify

00:02:45.395 --> 00:02:50.880
the problem at the start, and you slowly refine the problem, which is realistic because that sometimes users,

00:02:50.880 --> 00:02:51.520
you know,

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:57.360
don't know exactly all of what they want as they start the conversation, and they have to refine their intent over time.

00:02:58.800 --> 00:03:00.800
So we applied this idea

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:04.845
of, you know, sharding of instructions not only to code problems

00:03:05.085 --> 00:03:10.205
based on human eval, but also on other kinds of tasks that people might want might wanna do.

00:03:10.205 --> 00:03:14.765
So we did database task and actions around, you know,

00:03:15.245 --> 00:03:19.740
tool calling, and then we did math problems, data to text, and summarization.

00:03:19.740 --> 00:03:24.700
And these, like, you know, span from programming language to natural language tasks, all generation tasks.

00:03:25.580 --> 00:03:31.260
And basically, we took these instructions that exist that are high quality from existing datasets, and then we

00:03:30.655 --> 00:03:33.135
implemented the sharding process here shown in yellow.

00:03:34.575 --> 00:03:38.495
And from there, we, you know, implemented what we call a simulation

00:03:38.895 --> 00:03:41.055
environment that, you know, takes in

00:03:41.375 --> 00:03:42.575
a sharded instruction

00:03:42.790 --> 00:03:45.270
and can simulate a multi turn conversation

00:03:45.350 --> 00:03:50.390
by leveraging a small language model at the time we use GPT four o mini to kind of decide, okay. At

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:53.110
each point in the conversation when the assistant has responded,

00:03:53.270 --> 00:03:58.435
we have a little decider that can choose, okay. Which shard should I reveal next? And, essentially, the

00:03:58.435 --> 00:04:03.635
idea is to reveal at most one one new chart of information per turn so that the conversations are multi

00:04:03.635 --> 00:04:05.075
turn and underspecified.

00:04:06.355 --> 00:04:07.075
Alright.

00:04:07.315 --> 00:04:12.470
So with this in mind, you know, you might be wondering, okay. Like, I think I know where this is

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:17.590
going. You're gonna tell me that the, you know, sharded multi turn performance in the underspecified setting is gonna

00:04:17.590 --> 00:04:22.710
perform worse than the fully specified single turn. And one idea you might have is

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:25.590
maybe this loss is due to the actual sharding process.

00:04:26.045 --> 00:04:31.085
When you shard the information in, you know, an instruction into multiple shards, if you're not

00:04:31.085 --> 00:04:34.045
careful, you might lose some information during the sharding,

00:04:34.125 --> 00:04:39.005
and that might make the task infeasible in the multi turn setting. Right? So we have to be careful about this.

00:04:39.720 --> 00:04:45.080
And because of this, we implemented what we call the concat experiment, and let me show you what that looks like.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:49.640
The idea is we're gonna simulate know, given a fully specified instruction

00:04:49.720 --> 00:04:51.320
and its sharded equivalent,

00:04:51.400 --> 00:04:53.400
we're gonna have three ways of conducting,

00:04:54.435 --> 00:04:56.675
you know, a simulated conversation.

00:04:57.155 --> 00:05:02.435
The full conversation is just the we just provide the, you know, single instruction at the start,

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:05.235
the original one, and then let the assistant complete the task.

00:05:05.810 --> 00:05:09.810
Sharded is as we said. You know, we're gonna shard the converse the the instruction

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:15.090
and then give one shard per turn. And then concat, the idea is we're

00:05:15.090 --> 00:05:16.690
gonna take the shards

00:05:16.850 --> 00:05:22.225
of the sharded instructions, put them in a bullet list, and provide all of that information in a single turn.

00:05:22.305 --> 00:05:27.425
So now, hopefully, you'll see that, like, the concat versus full, they're both single turn,

00:05:27.425 --> 00:05:30.705
and the only difference is the rephrasing that has occurred during sharding.

00:05:30.945 --> 00:05:32.385
And then concat versus sharding

00:05:32.880 --> 00:05:36.880
sharded, they're exactly the same words, the same paraphrasing,

00:05:36.960 --> 00:05:39.520
except the only difference now is single versus multiturn.

00:05:39.520 --> 00:05:45.120
So, hopefully, concat is gonna, you know, kind of separate the the the effect from the sharding rephrasing

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:45.760
that has occurred.

00:05:47.645 --> 00:05:52.685
This is the main results of the paper, and, hopefully, you can see that, you know, there's the long story short is that

00:05:52.685 --> 00:05:57.885
we find that, you know, given the performance here on the left of, you know, one model per row

00:05:57.885 --> 00:06:03.020
and on the six tasks that we that we experimented with, you know, we can see that the full

00:06:03.020 --> 00:06:08.140
performance and the concat performance are mostly the same. You can see the colors are mostly white. Some of the

00:06:08.140 --> 00:06:13.420
older and and smaller models perform a little worse in concat, but the top models that we tested

00:06:13.740 --> 00:06:18.845
do pretty good and pretty similarly in full and concat. On the other hand, there's large degradation that

00:06:18.845 --> 00:06:23.645
we that we observe when we go into the sharded, you know, underspecified multi return setup.

00:06:25.165 --> 00:06:29.565
Okay? And then let me now get into why do models get lost in conversation.

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:35.730
So we actually for each, you know, simulates for each instruction that we have,

00:06:35.730 --> 00:06:37.890
we actually ran 10 simulations

00:06:37.890 --> 00:06:39.010
instead of one.

00:06:39.570 --> 00:06:44.530
And what we're interested in in seeing, you know, not only the average performance, which is what we saw in the table above,

00:06:44.855 --> 00:06:50.135
but also two new metrics that we came up with. One is called aptitude, and it corresponds to basically

00:06:50.135 --> 00:06:54.615
the best case performance for any given instruction, which is the ninetieth percentile performance,

00:06:55.815 --> 00:06:57.175
and also unreliability,

00:06:57.175 --> 00:06:59.015
which is the the gap between

00:06:59.255 --> 00:07:03.700
best and worst case performance. So the gap between ninetieth and tenth percentile.

00:07:04.980 --> 00:07:09.780
And the observation that we have is that, you know, the degradation in performance that we observe

00:07:10.740 --> 00:07:12.980
from blue to yellow here

00:07:13.455 --> 00:07:14.815
mostly occurs

00:07:14.895 --> 00:07:19.855
as a small drop in aptitude, meaning the best case performance drops a little bit, but an

00:07:19.935 --> 00:07:22.175
a very large increase in unreliability,

00:07:22.175 --> 00:07:24.495
meaning that the worst case and

00:07:24.655 --> 00:07:26.815
worst case to best case performance has dropped a lot.

00:07:27.470 --> 00:07:33.070
So the way we phrase this is that when LLMs take a wrong turn in a conversation, they get lost and do not recover.

00:07:33.070 --> 00:07:38.110
It's not that in multi turn settings, the models become incapable of completing certain tasks.

00:07:38.190 --> 00:07:43.310
It's just that there is more room for the models to get things wrong, which means that the unreliability has increased a lot

00:07:43.365 --> 00:07:44.245
of the system.

00:07:44.885 --> 00:07:45.525
Okay.

00:07:45.925 --> 00:07:48.325
Some additional experiments that we conducted.

00:07:48.885 --> 00:07:54.165
One is you might be thinking, okay. But what if we implement an agent that has memory and

00:07:54.165 --> 00:07:55.285
that can, like, say,

00:07:55.765 --> 00:08:00.860
recap the information that has occurred so far in the conversation, would that help reduce the amount

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:04.220
of, you know, information loss and degradation in performance?

00:08:04.380 --> 00:08:09.500
So we implemented two new kinds of simulations that you can see here in the diagram. One is called

00:08:09.500 --> 00:08:10.940
recap and one is called snowball.

00:08:11.265 --> 00:08:16.305
And both of them, essentially, you know, as we introduce new shards of information in the conversation, we also

00:08:16.305 --> 00:08:18.465
implement a system to, you know,

00:08:18.865 --> 00:08:21.745
repeat the information that has occurred so far in the conversation.

00:08:22.225 --> 00:08:27.680
And the finding from our experiment here on the right is what you can see is that, you know, that does improve

00:08:27.680 --> 00:08:33.200
performance compared to just plain sharded simulation, but it doesn't quite close the gap. So agents

00:08:33.200 --> 00:08:37.920
help a little bit, but they don't fundamentally solve the problem, at least the way we set it up.

00:08:39.465 --> 00:08:42.025
Another thing you might be thinking is what about temperature?

00:08:42.185 --> 00:08:42.985
Reliability

00:08:42.985 --> 00:08:47.545
of AI systems can sometimes be related to temperature because these systems are stochastic.

00:08:47.865 --> 00:08:52.425
So we tried reducing the temperature of both the assistant, this is on the columns,

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:54.470
and also the

00:08:54.790 --> 00:08:56.870
the user simulator

00:08:57.270 --> 00:08:59.430
here in the in the rows.

00:08:59.670 --> 00:09:02.310
And we find that in the singleton scenario,

00:09:02.710 --> 00:09:07.110
so this is concat in full, blowing temperature does help reduce unreliability.

00:09:07.895 --> 00:09:13.095
On the other hand, it has almost no effect in the multi turn setting. And that's because in multi turn

00:09:13.095 --> 00:09:18.135
setting, even a small token divergence in an early turn basically cascades and becomes entirely

00:09:18.135 --> 00:09:19.175
different conversations.

00:09:19.575 --> 00:09:24.580
So we find that decreasing temperature even to zero does not lead to reduced

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:26.660
unreliability in multiturn setups.

00:09:27.860 --> 00:09:29.780
We have some additional information

00:09:30.100 --> 00:09:33.860
in the paper about what are the reasons that models get lost.

00:09:34.180 --> 00:09:39.255
One of them is that the models make answer attempts too early even if they don't have all the elements

00:09:39.255 --> 00:09:44.695
of the problem. We find that, you know, the the later that the model makes an attempt,

00:09:44.695 --> 00:09:45.895
the better the performance.

00:09:46.615 --> 00:09:51.670
We we also find that there is this concept of answer blow that as the model goes deeper and deeper in the

00:09:51.670 --> 00:09:52.390
conversation,

00:09:52.710 --> 00:09:57.750
the models aren't very good at invalidating bad assumptions, and so their answers sort of tend to bloat.

00:09:57.750 --> 00:10:02.870
So even if you get a correct answer at the end of a multi turn conversation, it's bloated by 30 to 40% compared

00:10:02.870 --> 00:10:03.590
to single turn.

00:10:04.565 --> 00:10:10.005
We also find that there is a loss in middle turns phenomenon where basically

00:10:10.005 --> 00:10:15.285
the the models pay more attention to early information or late information but not

00:10:15.285 --> 00:10:16.085
middle information.

00:10:17.030 --> 00:10:20.390
And that models tend to produce overly verbose answers,

00:10:20.550 --> 00:10:25.590
which tends to confuse them later in the conversation because they get confused with prior assumptions that they

00:10:25.590 --> 00:10:27.030
made that were incorrect.

00:10:27.830 --> 00:10:29.510
That's it. Thanks for listening.

00:10:30.150 --> 00:10:31.590
The TLDR is that, you know,

00:10:32.425 --> 00:10:36.425
sharding instructions in a is a, you know, seemingly benign way

00:10:37.305 --> 00:10:42.345
that leads to catastrophic degradation in LLM performance on both natural language and programming language

00:10:42.345 --> 00:10:43.545
generation tasks.

00:10:43.865 --> 00:10:46.825
This manifests as a loss of reliability specifically,

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:50.420
and that means that models can still do everything sometimes,

00:10:50.660 --> 00:10:55.780
and, of course, this is bad for users. So the what we want you to remember is that when LLMs take a wrong

00:10:55.780 --> 00:10:59.540
turn, they get lost and do not recover. Thank you for listening.